+# Nested Vehicle Ecology, Docking, Deployment, and Base-Carrier Runtime
+
+## Runtime Purpose
+
+Support a persistent hierarchy in which personal vehicles, mobile homes, surface bases, submarines, landers, tenders and medium carriers can embark aboard larger carriers without losing identity, residents, cargo, damage, ownership or agency.
+
+> **A carrier carries homes and institutions, not anonymous vehicle slots.**
+
+# 1. Nested Mobility Graph
+
+Every transportable entity may expose one or more carriage relationships.
+
+```text
+Civilization Carrier
+├── orbital base carrier
+│   ├── atmospheric lander
+│   │   ├── Continuance crawler
+│   │   │   ├── scout bikes
+│   │   │   └── field drones
+│   │   └── deployable clinic
+│   └── utility shuttles
+├── ocean interface carrier
+│   ├── flotilla homes
+│   ├── expedition submarines
+│   │   └── dive pods
+│   └── seafloor habitat modules
+└── independent convoy partners
+```
+
+The graph must remain acyclic in physical carriage state. Ownership, command and communications graphs may be more complex.
+
+# 2. Vehicle Identity Preservation
+
+Embarkation must preserve:
+
+- vehicle identity;
+- home lineage;
+- residents and guests;
+- inventories and provenance;
+- damage;
+- maintenance debt;
+- pressure and contamination state;
+- power and thermal state;
+- legal ownership and liens;
+- operational authority;
+- attached subvehicles;
+- personal and cultural modifications;
+- Chronicle history.
+
+The carried vehicle is not converted into a generic cargo record unless it is genuinely disassembled under a recorded process.
+
+# 3. Carriage Modes
+
+## 3.1 Internal hangar
+
+The craft enters a protected volume.
+
+Advantages:
+
+- environmental protection;
+- pressurized access;
+- comprehensive maintenance;
+- rapid cargo exchange.
+
+Costs:
+
+- enormous volume;
+- pressure and fire risk;
+- door mass;
+- traffic bottlenecks.
+
+## 3.2 External hardpoint
+
+The craft attaches to the exterior structure.
+
+Advantages:
+
+- efficient volume use;
+- simple release;
+- flexible shapes.
+
+Costs:
+
+- radiation and debris exposure;
+- difficult access;
+- thermal coupling challenges;
+- visible vulnerability.
+
+## 3.3 Cradle or partial enclosure
+
+The carrier supports structural loads while the carried craft remains partly exposed.
+
+Useful for crawlers, submarines, aerostats and unusual hulls.
+
+## 3.4 Containerized or folded module
+
+A mobile base transforms or disassembles into standardized transport sections.
+
+Identity continuity requires explicit mapping from assembled home to packaged components.
+
+## 3.5 Towed or tethered formation
+
+The carrier provides propulsion, route coordination or shielding without rigid docking.
+
+Useful for large independent habitats and political partners unwilling to surrender physical autonomy.
+
+## 3.6 Symbiotic carriage
+
+A living or adaptive carrier grows a cradle, carries a smaller organism internally, or forms a temporary physiological bond.
+
+Consent and biological compatibility are required.
+
+# 4. Dock Compatibility
+
+A docking attempt evaluates:
+
+- geometry;
+- relative motion;
+- structural loads;
+- pressure standard;
+- atmosphere and contamination;
+- power compatibility;
+- thermal compatibility;
+- data trust;
+- legal permission;
+- traffic authority;
+- quarantine state;
+- crew readiness;
+- emergency abort paths.
+
+Compatibility is multidimensional.
+
+A craft may be:
+
+- mechanically docked but pressure-isolated;
+- structurally carried but electrically independent;
+- socially admitted but biologically quarantined;
+- technically compatible but refused by its owner;
+- physically incompatible but transferable through tender vehicles.
+
+# 5. Docking Workflow
+
+1. Request and identify.
+2. Exchange declared mass, dimensions, hazards and authority.
+3. Assign approach corridor.
+4. Match velocity and attitude.
+5. Soft capture.
+6. Verify alignment and structure.
+7. Hard capture or cradle lock.
+8. Establish selected utility links.
+9. Complete contamination and security checks.
+10. Transfer people, cargo or operational authority as authorized.
+11. Update carrier mass properties and emergency plan.
+
+Failures must be recoverable before hard capture whenever possible.
+
+# 6. Deployment Workflow
+
+Deployment is a planned operation rather than an instant spawn.
+
+It includes:
+
+- mission authorization;
+- crew and passenger manifest;
+- cargo loading;
+- center-of-mass planning;
+- fuel and reserve assignment;
+- environmental compatibility;
+- landing, ocean or orbital site assessment;
+- communications plan;
+- recovery window;
+- alternate destination;
+- quarantine conditions;
+- ownership and return obligations.
+
+A carried base can refuse deployment if its autonomous residents, owner or safety authority has standing under the charter.
+
+# 7. Smaller Base-Carrier Roles
+
+Medium carriers bridge civilization hulls and local mobile homes.
+
+They should support many roles while emphasizing one operating environment.
+
+## Planetary descent carrier
+
+Carries crawlers, aerostats, clinics, survey craft and deployable habitats through atmospheric interface.
+
+## Ocean base carrier
+
+Carries submarines, flotilla homes, dive systems, marine labs and seafloor construction modules.
+
+## Orbital tender
+
+Carries workboats, station pods, rescue craft, cargo movers and repair habitats.
+
+## Hostile-world insertion carrier
+
+Carries burrowers, hardened shelters, walkers, laboratories, rescue equipment and evacuation craft.
+
+## Convoy transport carrier
+
+Moves independent ground or maritime homes across otherwise impassable distances while preserving their local governance.
+
+Each medium carrier still requires care, fabrication, habitation, logistics and emergency capability.
+
+# 8. Carrier-of-Carrier Constraints
+
+Nested carriage creates multiplicative complexity.
+
+The runtime must account for:
+
+- total mass and inertia;
+- stacking and load paths;
+- launch and landing loads;
+- carried propellants and hazardous materials;
+- simultaneous maintenance demand;
+- emergency evacuation depth;
+- crew trapped several nesting levels away;
+- communications through pressure and electromagnetic boundaries;
+- fire and contamination propagation;
+- dock availability during damage;
+- deployment sequencing;
+- recovery dependencies.
+
+A vehicle should not be nested indefinitely merely because a bounding box fits.
+
+# 9. Mass and Balance
+
+Every docked or carried object updates:
+
+- center of mass;
+- inertia tensor;
+- structural load distribution;
+- propulsion trim;
+- attitude-control authority;
+- radiator shadowing;
+- sensor occlusion;
+- docking-corridor availability;
+- emergency separation behavior.
+
+Unsafe loading may be physically possible but legally refused.
+
+# 10. Traffic and Harbor Life
+
+A busy carrier harbor includes:
+
+- approach queues;
+- pilots and traffic controllers;
+- tugs;
+- loaders;
+- inspectors;
+- customs and health staff;
+- repair crews;
+- families awaiting arrivals;
+- markets and contractors;
+- independent captains negotiating berth time.
+
+Vehicle arrival should be social content, not only a loading screen.
+
+# 11. Ownership and Service Relationships
+
+Carriage does not transfer ownership.
+
+Possible agreements:
+
+- paid berth;
+- public-service allocation;
+- convoy membership;
+- rescue custody;
+- temporary impound;
+- maintenance contract;
+- military requisition with process;
+- refugee protection;
+- household affiliation;
+- cooperative membership;
+- reciprocal transport treaty.
+
+The carrier may have duties toward carried homes, including power, shelter, evacuation and timely release.
+
+# 12. Emergencies
+
+## Dock fire
+
+Requires isolation, firefighting, atmosphere management and decisions about jettison.
+
+## Pressure contamination
+
+May isolate craft and residents without criminalizing them.
+
+## Failed deployment
+
+A vehicle may remain attached, collide, lose power or land outside recovery range.
+
+## Carrier evacuation
+
+Smaller bases become lifeboats, but capacity, readiness and independent launch conditions matter.
+
+## Lost return window
+
+The carrier may wait, send rescue, depart under necessity or transfer responsibility to another institution.
+
+## Captured carried craft
+
+Identity, command keys and source-chain records persist; the vehicle does not become owned merely through physical control.
+
+# 13. Time Compression and Persistence
+
+Long embarkation and transit can be compressed after planning and initial execution.
+
+Compression must retain:
+
+- wear;
+- resource consumption;
+- crew work;
+- social events;
+- maintenance findings;
+- schedule deviation;
+- emergencies selected from actual state.
+
+# 14. Multiplayer Authority
+
+- Docking state is authoritative at the locality hosting the physical interface.
+- Owners and commanders may be different players or NPC institutions.
+- Departure requires resolved physical and legal authority.
+- A disconnected player cannot freeze a carrier by occupying a berth.
+- Safe delegation and absence rules preserve character rights without blocking the whole community.
+- Host migration cannot duplicate carried craft.
+
+# 15. First Vertical Slice
+
+Prove one chain:
+
+```text
+large orbital carrier
+→ descent base-carrier
+→ Continuance crawler
+→ field rover
+```
+
+The slice should support:
+
+- embarkation;
+- persistent interiors;
+- mass update;
+- player movement across levels;
+- one deployment;
+- one aborted deployment;
+- return and maintenance;
+- independent crawler ownership;
+- carrier charter authority;
+- exact replay.
+
+## Runtime Maxim
+
+> **The smallest home remains itself even when it is carried inside a machine large enough to contain a city.**
